{
  "dom_id": "step-q335121",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x):$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=2x-z,\\, & y(-1)=1 \\\\ {z}^{′}=x+y,\\, & z(-1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(0)$ and $z(0).$\n\n$y(0)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(0)≈$\n{{field-2}}",
  "worked_solution": "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z$ at $x=-1$ and asked to find their values at $x=0.$ The distance from $x=-1$ to $x=0$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{0-(-1)}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(-1)=1$ and $z(-1)=0.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x-z=2\\cdot (-1)-0=-2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =x+y=-1+1=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=-1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=-1+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+(-1)=0 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x-z=2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})-0=-1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =x+y=-\\frac{1}{2}+0=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=0 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+(-\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+(-\\frac{1}{4})=-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(0)≈-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{and}\\,z(0)≈-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$",
